1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:05,180
11 p .m. July 9th, 1962. 250 miles

2
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:08,920
above the Pacific, the United States detonates a nuclear bomb in space.

3
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:12,940
A newspaper in Hawaii wrote it plainly the next morning.

4
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:17,020
It looked as though the heavens had belched forth a new sun that flared briefly,

5
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:19,340
but long enough to set the sky on fire.

6
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:23,700
Two seconds later, 900 miles away in Honolulu,

7
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:25,500
streetlights start going out.

8
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:28,900
Nobody in Hawaii had been told a bomb was about to go off in space.

9
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:32,860
This is the real story of the day America nuked space,

10
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:34,660
the actual damage it did,

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,180
the satellite it quietly killed months later,

12
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:41,300
and why the exact same weapon is the reason the Pentagon is worried again right now.

13
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:43,800
You're watching Mind Wired.

14
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,560
This is the full real story of Starfish Prime,

15
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:51,060
the actual Cold War test that put a nuclear bomb in space,

16
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:54,200
the real damage it caused on the ground and in orbit,

17
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:58,920
and the live 2024 discovery that made this 60 -year -old test relevant again.

18
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:03,080
Five years earlier, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik,

19
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:07,000
and the United States panicked about a threat nobody had ever had to plan for,

20
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:08,960
nuclear missiles coming from space.

21
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:15,700
In 1958, physicist Nicholas Christophillos theorized that a nuclear explosion could trap

22
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:17,560
electrons in Earth's magnetic field,

23
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:19,140
creating an artificial shield.

24
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:22,380
The military tested his idea over the South Atlantic.

25
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:24,500
It was called Operation Argus.

26
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:27,760
A three -year moratorium on testing followed.

27
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:31,280
It ended when the Soviets resumed testing in 1961,

28
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:34,200
capping it with the largest nuclear weapon ever built.

29
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:37,140
The United States resumed testing almost immediately.

30
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:40,860
The American answer was Operation Fishbowl,

31
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:46,120
a series of high -altitude nuclear tests launched from a tiny American military outpost

32
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:48,440
in the middle of the Pacific called Johnston Island.

33
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:53,160
Nobody had ever deliberately detonated a nuclear weapon this high before.

34
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,000
The honest goal was simple and unsettling.

35
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:58,060
Find out what actually happens,

36
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:00,460
because right now, nobody really knows.

37
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:04,820
The plan, launch a Thor missile from Johnston Island,

38
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:10,840
carrying a warhead with roughly the explosive power of 1 .4 million tons of TNT and

39
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:13,200
detonated 250 miles above the Earth.

40
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:16,660
The first attempt, three weeks earlier,

41
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:21,720
failed. The Thor missile broke apart less than a minute after launch and had to be destroyed

42
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:26,120
in the air, scattering radioactive debris back onto the island that launched it.

43
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:29,060
Three weeks later, they tried again.

44
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:31,200
This time, the missile flew.

45
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:33,920
It climbed for several minutes,

46
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:36,220
arced 250 miles above the Pacific,

47
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:39,180
and the warhead detonated exactly as planned.

48
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:43,260
250 miles up, over the open Pacific,

49
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:47,200
the warhead detonated. Close to the blast,

50
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:50,960
observers reported a brilliant white flash burning through the clouds,

51
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:54,440
changing almost instantly into an expanding green ball of light,

52
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:58,220
then white streaks, then a full artificial aurora in green,

53
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:01,240
purple, and red. It lasted seven minutes.

54
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,100
From an island 2 ,600 kilometers away,

55
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:07,020
the display lasted 90 minutes.

56
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:10,680
The flash itself was visible as far as Fiji and New Zealand,

57
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:13,160
more than 3 ,000 kilometers from the blast.

58
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:17,320
In Honolulu, 1 ,400 kilometers away,

59
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:19,600
the effects arrived almost instantly.

60
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:23,180
The electromagnetic pulse hit within a fraction of a second.

61
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:26,300
About 300 streetlights went dark.

62
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:29,680
Burglar alarms across the city started going off for no reason.

63
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:32,940
A telephone company relay link between the islands was damaged,

64
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:40,060
cutting phone service. It

65
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:41,780
reached one controlling a whole string of lights.

66
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:45,520
But the real explanation is almost more unsettling than the myth.

67
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:50,900
A bomb detonated in space had reached down and quietly broke in specific pieces of equipment

68
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:53,360
on the ground from outside the atmosphere entirely.

69
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:56,680
No one in Hawaii had been evacuated,

70
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:59,000
warned, or told to expect anything.

71
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:02,840
The only sign that anything had happened at all was the sky itself,

72
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:07,420
and a handful of streetlights going dark for no reason anyone on the ground could see.

73
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:10,720
The blast didn't just light up the sky.

74
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:13,880
It created something that had never existed before.

75
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:19,380
An artificial belt of radiation wrapped around the earth that would outlast the people

76
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:22,760
who built it by decades. Within days,

77
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:25,240
a small American research satellite failed.

78
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:28,100
Within months, Britain's first satellite,

79
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:31,880
Ariel 1, and America's Transit 4B were dead too.

80
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:36,100
Their solar panels quietly destroyed by radiation that shouldn't have existed.

81
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:41,640
Roughly a third of everything humanity had in orbit at the time was damaged by a single

82
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:45,800
test. In 1962, that was a small number of satellites.

83
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:47,940
Today, it would be thousands.

84
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:53,660
Some of the radiation from that one bomb was still detectable in Earth's magnetic field

85
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:55,960
five years later. By some estimates,

86
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:57,800
traces lasted almost a decade.

87
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:00,860
The day after Starfish Prime,

88
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:04,920
a different rocket carried the most important satellite of the decade into orbit,

89
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:08,740
Telstar 1, the world's first active communications satellite,

90
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:12,920
the machine that led America and Europe exchange a live television signal for the first

91
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:15,740
time in history. For months,

92
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:17,160
Telstar worked perfectly.

93
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:21,100
Then, in November, its command system started glitching.

94
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:23,520
By February of the following year,

95
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:27,740
it was permanently dead. It wasn't destroyed by the blast.

96
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:31,560
It was slowly poisoned by the radiation belt the blast created,

97
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:33,620
one silent dose at a time,

98
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:37,620
for seven months until the damage finally added up to total failure.

99
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:43,160
The most important communications satellite of the century and the bomb that would slowly

100
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:47,760
kill it launched one day apart without either American program knowing what the other

101
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:52,960
would do to it. A test that broke streetlights 900 miles away and quietly

102
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:57,300
killed satellites for months afterward became one of the clearest arguments that testing

103
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:02,120
nuclear weapons in the atmosphere and in space had consequences nobody could contain.

104
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:04,600
It wasn't the only reason.

105
00:06:05,020 --> 00:06:06,000
The Cuban missile crisis,

106
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:10,700
three months later, and growing public fear over radioactive fallout did most of the

107
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:13,600
political work. But Starfish Prime was real,

108
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:17,900
undeniable proof, a nuclear test in space doesn't stay in space.

109
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:21,240
One year later, the United States,

110
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:25,880
the Soviet Union, and Britain signed a treaty banning nuclear tests in the atmosphere

111
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:31,200
underwater and in space. Nobody has detonated a nuclear weapon in space since.

112
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:37,780
Every officer, engineer, and observer who was actually there in 1962 is

113
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:42,620
gone now. No one who witnessed Starfish Prime firsthand is alive to describe it anymore.

114
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:48,560
But in February of 2024, American intelligence officials told Congress something that

115
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:50,420
made Starfish Prime relevant again.

116
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:54,560
Russia is developing a nuclear weapon designed to be detonated in space.

117
00:06:56,040 --> 00:07:01,080
In 1962, a test like this damaged a third of the roughly two dozen satellites

118
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:03,320
humanity had in orbit. Today,

119
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:07,940
one company alone has thousands of satellites in the same kind of orbit that Starfish

120
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:09,220
Prime poisoned for years.

121
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:14,660
Every analysis of what a modern version of this weapon would do starts in the same place.

122
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:17,140
The real data from July 9th,

123
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:22,220
1962. The one time anyone actually did this and measured what happened.

124
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:28,480
62 years later, a decision made in the middle of the Cold War is the closest thing anyone

125
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:31,280
has to a real answer for a threat that hasn't happened yet.

126
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:35,720
A bomb went off in space and 900 miles away,

127
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:40,360
streetlights went dark for a reason almost nobody living can now explain from memory.

128
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:47,000
62 years later, the only complete data anyone has on what that actually does still comes

129
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:50,860
from one test, one warhead and one night over the Pacific.

130
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:55,900
If a test this old is still the reason satellites might be at risk today,

131
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:59,860
wait until you see the story where the danger never actually went away at all.

